    <h2>Willingness-to-Pay Analysis</h2>
    <p>We asked each participant how much they would pay annually to solve their biggest frustrations (Q22). On average, <strong>BambooHR customers would pay $18,714</strong>, <strong>Gusto customers $7,345</strong>, and <strong>Rippling customers $16,589</strong>. But those averages mask important differences in what drives that willingness to pay. Because participants cited multiple frustrations but gave one WTP figure, we ran a linear regression for each vendor to isolate how each frustration independently moves WTP up or down from the baseline. Positive coefficients mean that frustration drives buyers to pay more. Negative coefficients mean it is a commodity complaint that does not increase willingness to pay. Filled dots are statistically significant (p &lt; 0.05).<br/><span class="q-cite">Q18: {questionText.Q18}</span><br/><span class="q-cite">Q22: {questionText.Q22}</span></p>
    <p class="avg-legend" style="margin-top:-4px;font-size:11px;color:var(--muted);">Note: "Reporting Inadequacy" and "Reporting Gaps Undermining Strategic Role" were combined into a single "Reporting Gaps" predictor due to high collinearity (r = 0.94). Frustrations cited by fewer than 5 participants are excluded.</p>

    {[
      { title: "BambooHR Customers", reg: d.wtp_reg_bamboo, color: "#73C41D", bgColor: "rgba(115, 196, 29, 0.10)", yMin: -10000, yMax: 2000 },
      { title: "Gusto Customers", reg: d.wtp_reg_gusto, color: "#3B7DDD", bgColor: "rgba(59, 125, 221, 0.10)", yMin: -4000, yMax: 10000 },
      { title: "Rippling Customers", reg: d.wtp_reg_rippling, color: "#9B59B6", bgColor: "rgba(155, 89, 182, 0.10)", yMin: -4000, yMax: 8000 },
    ].map((chart: any) => {
      const minX = 0;
      const maxX = 65;
      const minY = chart.yMin;
      const maxY = chart.yMax;
      const pad = { top: 30, right: 40, bottom: 55, left: 80 };
      const w = 780;
      const h = 440;
      const plotW = w - pad.left - pad.right;
      const plotH = h - pad.top - pad.bottom;
      const sx = (v: number) => pad.left + ((v - minX) / (maxX - minX)) * plotW;
      const sy = (v: number) => pad.top + plotH - ((v - minY) / (maxY - minY)) * plotH;
      const xTicks = [0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60];
      // Build Y ticks dynamically based on range
      const yStep = 2000;
      const yTicks: number[] = [];
      for (let t = Math.ceil(minY / yStep) * yStep; t <= maxY; t += yStep) { yTicks.push(t); }
      return (
        <Fragment>
          <h3>{chart.title} (n={chart.reg.n}, R&sup2; = {chart.reg.r2})</h3>
          <p style="margin:-4px 0 8px;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);">Average WTP: <strong style="color:var(--ink);font-size:15px;">${chart.reg.intercept.toLocaleString()}</strong> per year. The chart below shows how each frustration moves that baseline up or down.</p>
          <div class="scatter-wrap">
            <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${w} ${h}`} class="scatter-svg">
              {/* Upper half highlight (positive coef region) */}
              <rect x={sx(20)} y={sy(maxY)} width={sx(maxX) - sx(20)} height={sy(0) - sy(maxY)} fill={chart.bgColor} />
              {/* Grid lines */}
              {xTicks.map((t: number) => <line x1={sx(t)} y1={sy(minY)} x2={sx(t)} y2={sy(maxY)} stroke="#e0ddd5" stroke-width="0.5" />)}
              {yTicks.map((t: number) => <line x1={sx(minX)} y1={sy(t)} x2={sx(maxX)} y2={sy(t)} stroke="#e0ddd5" stroke-width="0.5" />)}
              {/* Zero line (prominent) */}
              <line x1={sx(minX)} y1={sy(0)} x2={sx(maxX)} y2={sy(0)} stroke="#0B1F3A" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6,3" />
              <text x={sx(maxX) + 4} y={sy(0) + 4} font-size="10" fill="#6B6B6B">$0</text>
              {/* Quadrant labels */}
              <text x={sx(42)} y={sy(maxY) + 14} text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill={chart.color}>High-Value Opportunities</text>
              <text x={sx(42)} y={sy(minY) - 6} text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="500" fill="#6B6B6B">Commodity Complaints (don't move budget)</text>
              {/* X-axis tick labels */}
              {xTicks.map((t: number) => <text x={sx(t)} y={sy(minY) + 16} text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#6B6B6B">{t}%</text>)}
              {/* X-axis title */}
              <text x={sx((minX + maxX) / 2)} y={h - 6} text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="600" fill="#0B1F3A">% of Buyers Citing Frustration</text>
              {/* Y-axis tick labels */}
              {yTicks.map((t: number) => <text x={pad.left - 8} y={sy(t) + 4} text-anchor="end" font-size="11" fill="#6B6B6B">{t >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${(t / 1000).toFixed(0)}K</text>)}
              {/* Y-axis title */}
              <text x="18" y={sy((minY + maxY) / 2)} text-anchor="middle" font-size="16" font-weight="600" fill="#0B1F3A" transform={`rotate(-90, 18, ${sy((minY + maxY) / 2)})`}>Marginal WTP Contribution</text>
              {/* Axes */}
              <line x1={sx(minX)} y1={sy(minY)} x2={sx(maxX)} y2={sy(minY)} stroke="#0B1F3A" stroke-width="1" />
              <line x1={sx(minX)} y1={sy(minY)} x2={sx(minX)} y2={sy(maxY)} stroke="#0B1F3A" stroke-width="1" />
              {/* Data points (no error bars) */}
              {chart.reg.points.map((pt: any) => {
                const cx = sx(Math.min(pt.prevalence, maxX));
                const coefClamped = Math.max(minY, Math.min(pt.coef, maxY));
                const cy = sy(coefClamped);
                const isPositive = pt.coef > 0;
                const dotColor = pt.sig ? (isPositive ? chart.color : "#B8341B") : "#B0ADA5";
                const labelAbove = pt.coef >= 0;
                return (
                  <Fragment>
                    <circle cx={cx} cy={cy} r={pt.sig ? 8 : 6} fill={dotColor} opacity={pt.sig ? 0.85 : 0.4} />
                    <text x={cx + 12} y={labelAbove ? cy - 8 : cy + 16} font-size={pt.sig ? "11" : "10"} fill={pt.sig ? "#0B1F3A" : "#6B6B6B"} font-weight={pt.sig ? "600" : "400"}>{pt.label}</text>
                    <text x={cx + 12} y={labelAbove ? cy + 4 : cy + 28} font-size="9" fill={pt.sig ? "#0B1F3A" : "#999"}>{pt.coef >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${(pt.coef / 1000).toFixed(1)}K{pt.sig ? '*' : ''}</text>
                  </Fragment>
                );
              })}
            </svg>
          </div>
        </Fragment>
      );
    })}
    <p class="source-note">Source: OLS regression of Q22 (willingness to pay) on Q18 frustration flags. n=60 per platform. *p &lt; 0.05.</p>

    <div class="opp-callout">
      <p><strong>The frustrations that drive WTP differ sharply by vendor.</strong> BambooHR customers start with the highest baseline WTP ($18.7K), but their most common frustrations (payroll friction, benefits admin) are associated with lower WTP, suggesting these are table-stakes expectations. Gusto customers start low ($7.3K) but reporting gaps (+$6.2K, sig) and onboarding pain (+$6.9K, sig) drive significant WTP increases, representing genuine premium opportunities. Rippling customers have a high baseline ($16.6K) but no individual frustration significantly moves WTP, consistent with a customer base whose complaints are more diffuse.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Integration Strategy</h2>
    <p>Given BambooHR's 70% fragmented-stack customer base, integration is not a nice-to-have. It is the defensive investment that prevents the base from migrating to Rippling.</p>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Integration Area</th><th>Rationale</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>Payroll (native or deep integration)</td><td>67% cite payroll/HRIS friction as a shortcoming (Q17). 52% name it their biggest frustration (Q18). This is the #1 integration priority.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Benefits administration</td><td>20% of BambooHR users cite benefits friction (Q17). Separate benefits systems are common in the fragmented stack.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>Recruiting / ATS</td><td>Recruiting module inadequacy appears in Q17 and Q15 rejections. A native or deeply integrated ATS would close a competitive gap.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Performance management</td><td>45% of BambooHR users cite performance management as a gap (Q17). This is an emerging module opportunity.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </div>
</details>

